(828451, 91581, 22734, '<td valign="top">A very cool shot well composed with wonderful color and humor.</td>', 'A very cool shot well composed with wonderful color and humor.', '2004-07-27 23:01:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(828466, 91452, 12611, '<td valign="top">This has a great feel of a summer lake adn the  stillness of glass. There is a remarkable feeling of isolation, fear, danger and yet calmness. It makes you want to hop forward onto the float and just do a plunge over the back slowly. There is also a subtle religious overtone that is only solidified by the light. Another great shot Mona.</td>', 'This has a great feel of a summer lake adn the  stillness of glass. There is a remarkable feeling of isolation, fear, danger and yet calmness. It makes you want to hop forward onto the float and just do a plunge over the back slowly. There is also a subtle religious overtone that is only solidified by the light. Another great shot Mona.', '2004-07-27 23:15:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(828771, 91987, 6349, '<td valign="top">If I ever get married I''m hiring you.  :)</td>', 'If I ever get married I''m hiring you.  :)', '2004-07-28 01:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(829192, 91619, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/>...............................................................<br/> <br/>Beautiful form, lighting and focus makes this a very attractive image indeed. The composition is sound (perhaps a little too tight on the right) and the subject is indeed well balanced. Of course, nature so often is. The reason this did not score so well in the challenge is probably down to the fact that the shot does not sing of ''balance'' per se. Most people would ask "I wonder what theme this represents?" if unaware of the challenge brief, and their answer may be ''Beauty'', ''Grace'', ''Form'', etc.<br/> <br/>If I was pushed for sugggestions to improve this I would have tried to achieve a longer DOF and increase the contrast between the background and the main flower itself. Also, given your explanation of its size, a suggestion of the scale might be good for your subject (but would not have helped your placing in this challenge) - and the border is definitely quite overpowering for such delicacy.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! ............................................................... Beautiful form, lighting and focus makes this a very attractive image indeed. The composition is sound (perhaps a little too tight on the right) and the subject is indeed well balanced. Of course, nature so often is. The reason this did not score so well in the challenge is probably down to the fact that the shot does not sing of ''balance'' per se. Most people would ask "I wonder what theme this represents?" if unaware of the challenge brief, and their answer may be ''Beauty'', ''Grace'', ''Form'', etc. If I was pushed for sugggestions to improve this I would have tried to achieve a longer DOF and increase the contrast between the background and the main flower itself. Also, given your explanation of its size, a suggestion of the scale might be good for your subject (but would not have helped your placing in this challenge) - and the border is definitely quite overpowering for such delicacy.', '2004-07-28 05:43:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(830540, 91033, 21183, '<td valign="top">This is a very awe inspiring photo.  I would love to learn how to take some photos of reflective surfaces that don''t include my camera and my reflection in it. If you get the time - please share the ''tip''?  <br/>Well done on the blue ribbon. Very deserving. The artistry of the photo is incredible. Not only does it seem to answer the challenge of ''balance'', but those orbs almost appear like eyes!  I''m also impressed with the colours in the photo.</td>', 'This is a very awe inspiring photo.  I would love to learn how to take some photos of reflective surfaces that don''t include my camera and my reflection in it. If you get the time - please share the ''tip''?  Well done on the blue ribbon. Very deserving. The artistry of the photo is incredible. Not only does it seem to answer the challenge of ''balance'', but those orbs almost appear like eyes!  I''m also impressed with the colours in the photo.', '2004-07-28 18:33:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(830671, 91297, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello From the Critique Club <br/> <br/>Im not sure which is your first name Aznym or Adam (so forgive me for that)<br/> <br/>Your image has very good detail and sharpness, focus good <br/>I also like the Black and white, good choice<br/>Overall a good image <br/>To me, however, it doesn''t say ''Balance'' as the number of windows are uneven each side.<br/>If you had cropped it so that, either two or three windows were each side and had the corner of the building in the centre it would even it up<br/>Also the way you have the top cropped on one side makes it appear uneven<br/>Another idea from me is maybe if you had taken a photo of just the windows all the same in a row it would be balanced ( just a thought for next time)as the windows are interesting in themselves <br/> <br/>So technically a good image but I think the reason why it scored average to low was that it just does not say ''balance'' and that''s what the people wanted.<br/>There was also some very good images in this challenge <br/>The idea with D P C is the ''Wow'' factor that gets the votes I M H O <br/>There are many really great images that score average<br/>So I hope this helps and if you have any questions you can P M  me <br/>Keep up the good work!<br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello From the Critique Club  Im not sure which is your first name Aznym or Adam (so forgive me for that) Your image has very good detail and sharpness, focus good I also like the Black and white, good choiceOverall a good image To me, however, it doesn''t say ''Balance'' as the number of windows are uneven each side.If you had cropped it so that, either two or three windows were each side and had the corner of the building in the centre it would even it upAlso the way you have the top cropped on one side makes it appear unevenAnother idea from me is maybe if you had taken a photo of just the windows all the same in a row it would be balanced ( just a thought for next time)as the windows are interesting in themselves  So technically a good image but I think the reason why it scored average to low was that it just does not say ''balance'' and that''s what the people wanted.There was also some very good images in this challenge The idea with D P C is the ''Wow'' factor that gets the votes I M H O There are many really great images that score averageSo I hope this helps and if you have any questions you can P M  me Keep up the good work!RegardsSally', '2004-07-28 19:58:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(830700, 91772, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Steven from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>Your image is interesting but the over all effect is dull <br/>It is the light effect that does that I think which is a shame as you have gone to a whole lot of effort to produce this <br/> <br/>The white background looks greyish and that takes a lot from the image if you could backlight this I think it could stand out better or placed the image on a dark background maybe black and covered the whole tabletop with the same cloth it would appear to be balancing in space if you can understand what I mean.<br/>Also there is a lot of reflection on the shaker which detracts from the balancing act and is a little soft in the focus this need to be really sharp with the blade<br/>A very good effort at such a difficult image<br/>I hope my comments help and if you have any questions you can contact me by P M <br/>Good luck in your future challenges<br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Steven from the Critique Club  Your image is interesting but the over all effect is dull It is the light effect that does that I think which is a shame as you have gone to a whole lot of effort to produce this  The white background looks greyish and that takes a lot from the image if you could backlight this I think it could stand out better or placed the image on a dark background maybe black and covered the whole tabletop with the same cloth it would appear to be balancing in space if you can understand what I mean.Also there is a lot of reflection on the shaker which detracts from the balancing act and is a little soft in the focus this need to be really sharp with the bladeA very good effort at such a difficult imageI hope my comments help and if you have any questions you can contact me by P M Good luck in your future challengesRegardsSally', '2004-07-28 20:15:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(830712, 91666, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hi Marc from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>Firstly congratulations on your placing 15th is very good<br/> <br/>I like your image very much although it appears quite small <br/>Idea, focus and detail all great<br/>It appears a little dark but then that just adds to the effect IMHO<br/>The only thing that I can think of (if I get real picky) is that you have chopped a little off the bottom cards and that is a little irritating <br/>Also there are some great images in this challenge <br/>So Marc what else can I say but to say keep up the great work you certainly have the talent and the abilty<br/>Sorry if this doesn''t seem much of a critique but as I said before <br/>Its a great image <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hi Marc from the Critique Club  Firstly congratulations on your placing 15th is very good I like your image very much although it appears quite small Idea, focus and detail all greatIt appears a little dark but then that just adds to the effect IMHOThe only thing that I can think of (if I get real picky) is that you have chopped a little off the bottom cards and that is a little irritating Also there are some great images in this challenge So Marc what else can I say but to say keep up the great work you certainly have the talent and the abiltySorry if this doesn''t seem much of a critique but as I said before Its a great image RegardsSally', '2004-07-28 20:30:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(830988, 91033, 23923, '<td valign="top">AMAZING, GREAT PICTURE</td>', 'AMAZING, GREAT PICTURE', '2004-07-28 23:14:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(831534, 91703, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Timothy from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>I really love these warm colours so straight away your image is attractive to me. The tones are lovely<br/>The detail and focus are also very good <br/> I see where you are going with this but the image itself does not say Balance to me and I think when you look at the top 10 you will see how people seem to vote<br/> If it is not obvious to a person on a quick glance people tend to vote mid to low if they don''t understand  the image<br/>So even though your image is very good and a lot of images are in so many of the challenges that do an average or below vote, its just not obvious that this was Balance without the title I doubt people would see that <br/>It is very hard to know what people will like  I get frustrated myself<br/>But keep up the good work you have obvious talent and remember to be true to yourself I hope this helps<br/>Good luck in your future challenges <br/>Regards<br/>Sally<br/></td>', 'Hello Timothy from the Critique Club  I really love these warm colours so straight away your image is attractive to me. The tones are lovelyThe detail and focus are also very good  I see where you are going with this but the image itself does not say Balance to me and I think when you look at the top 10 you will see how people seem to vote If it is not obvious to a person on a quick glance people tend to vote mid to low if they don''t understand  the imageSo even though your image is very good and a lot of images are in so many of the challenges that do an average or below vote, its just not obvious that this was Balance without the title I doubt people would see that It is very hard to know what people will like  I get frustrated myselfBut keep up the good work you have obvious talent and remember to be true to yourself I hope this helpsGood luck in your future challenges RegardsSally', '2004-07-29 06:03:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(831563, 92176, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Patsy from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>This is truly a balancing act.A great find. Well done for that!<br/>Your placing in the challenge is also very good. <br/>The only thing I think may have helped here, (in my opinion) is I wonder what this would look like in Black and white instead of the colour as that is what seems to be the problem with the D P C voters <br/>It does look wierd around the sky level and the trees look almost too green <br/>Maybe change to B/W and play around with the tonal range to give more dramatic effect. Although the image itself IS very dramatic<br/> <br/>I dont think I can add much more to this  A great image!<br/>I hope my comments help if you have any questions you can P M me <br/>Good luck in the challenges<br/> Regards<br/>Sally<br/></td>', 'Hello Patsy from the Critique Club  This is truly a balancing act.A great find. Well done for that!Your placing in the challenge is also very good. The only thing I think may have helped here, (in my opinion) is I wonder what this would look like in Black and white instead of the colour as that is what seems to be the problem with the D P C voters It does look wierd around the sky level and the trees look almost too green Maybe change to B/W and play around with the tonal range to give more dramatic effect. Although the image itself IS very dramatic I dont think I can add much more to this  A great image!I hope my comments help if you have any questions you can P M me Good luck in the challenges RegardsSally', '2004-07-29 06:19:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(831624, 91802, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hi Liz from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>First of all your image is very striking  a lovely sunset but maybe a little over saturated or sharpened just a bit too much<br/>The comments given are all in your favour but I think in this challenge the voters tended to vote for the images with Balance as an obvious image look at the top 10<br/>I think what we all have to remember when we vote is that there is usually more than one way to look at the challenge <br/>I myself did wrong with this challenge and placed an image that seemed more harmonious  as you have done with the balance of nature and I scored very low.<br/> <br/>So even though you only had an average score  I dont see this as a reflection on you but more of how the voters vote, as many really nice images are like yours, just that,  really nice images but not quite with that ''WOW'' factor of how the people see things.<br/>Very frustrating but keep trying and I think like us all we will one day get it right for the D P C voters. (One can only hope!)<br/>There seems to be a very high standard on D P C with some very good cameras and equipment and some very good ideas<br/> <br/>Your image also looks nice with the top (The darker band)cropped off <br/> <br/>I hope my comments are helpful to you<br/>If you have any questions you can P M me <br/>Good luck in future challenges<br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hi Liz from the Critique Club  First of all your image is very striking  a lovely sunset but maybe a little over saturated or sharpened just a bit too muchThe comments given are all in your favour but I think in this challenge the voters tended to vote for the images with Balance as an obvious image look at the top 10I think what we all have to remember when we vote is that there is usually more than one way to look at the challenge I myself did wrong with this challenge and placed an image that seemed more harmonious  as you have done with the balance of nature and I scored very low. So even though you only had an average score  I dont see this as a reflection on you but more of how the voters vote, as many really nice images are like yours, just that,  really nice images but not quite with that ''WOW'' factor of how the people see things.Very frustrating but keep trying and I think like us all we will one day get it right for the D P C voters. (One can only hope!)There seems to be a very high standard on D P C with some very good cameras and equipment and some very good ideas Your image also looks nice with the top (The darker band)cropped off  I hope my comments are helpful to youIf you have any questions you can P M me Good luck in future challengesRegardsSally', '2004-07-29 07:12:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(831637, 90783, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Charles from the Critique club<br/> <br/>After seeing your list of comments I feel very technically unqualified <br/>But I will try to give you my honest opinion for what it''s worth.<br/>You certainly made an effort to achieve this image  points to your wife also LOL! <br/> <br/>This is a very good idea and well executed and definately meets the challenge  but for some reason it lacks ''spark'' I think it may be the lighting,  also the negative space above has a very grey sort of washed out look. I think if this had been done on a dark background (Maybe dark green, as in the bank note or black) your image would have stood out more and bought more tone to the image The knife also looks dull not because of the focus but because of the light factor<br/>Maybe its the time of day that you shot this image <br/>But to me, that is the only thing I can see that may help you in future with this type of image. It has to be Striking ''WOW'' to win the voters of D P C over and it can be so frustrating when you have tried so hard<br/>Believe me I know from experience  <br/>So good luck with your future challenges  <br/>I hope this helps and if you have any questions please P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Charles from the Critique club After seeing your list of comments I feel very technically unqualified But I will try to give you my honest opinion for what it''s worth.You certainly made an effort to achieve this image  points to your wife also LOL!  This is a very good idea and well executed and definately meets the challenge  but for some reason it lacks ''spark'' I think it may be the lighting,  also the negative space above has a very grey sort of washed out look. I think if this had been done on a dark background (Maybe dark green, as in the bank note or black) your image would have stood out more and bought more tone to the image The knife also looks dull not because of the focus but because of the light factorMaybe its the time of day that you shot this image But to me, that is the only thing I can see that may help you in future with this type of image. It has to be Striking ''WOW'' to win the voters of D P C over and it can be so frustrating when you have tried so hardBelieve me I know from experience  So good luck with your future challenges  I hope this helps and if you have any questions please P M me RegardsSally', '2004-07-29 07:35:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(832223, 92107, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/>......................................................<br/> <br/>A formidable looking insect (bush cricket?). This is well focused and an interesting creature. <br/> <br/>As some have mentioned, the flash is quite harsh and, as such, this becomes more of a study of the subject than an attractive, vote-winning shot. The shadow obscuring the top of the critter is its main problem really, although the crop of the antenna is bit of a shame but perhaps unavoidable. It''s well composed with plenty of texture too.<br/> <br/>If indeed you were after votes with this, then you can learn from the score that many people either don''t like bugs or are looking for the ''perfect'' picture.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! ...................................................... A formidable looking insect (bush cricket?). This is well focused and an interesting creature.  As some have mentioned, the flash is quite harsh and, as such, this becomes more of a study of the subject than an attractive, vote-winning shot. The shadow obscuring the top of the critter is its main problem really, although the crop of the antenna is bit of a shame but perhaps unavoidable. It''s well composed with plenty of texture too. If indeed you were after votes with this, then you can learn from the score that many people either don''t like bugs or are looking for the ''perfect'' picture. CheersJon', '2004-07-29 16:16:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(832243, 91350, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/>......................................................<br/> <br/>When viewing challenge images I like to see shots which work outside of the competition - normally a sign that it''s a good shot regardless of votes. This probably suffers in that respect, mainly due to the skewed arrangement, blue colour cast and rather centred composition. The set-up clearly shows balance but it doesn''t engage the viewer much beyond this, which ultimately is what may win votes.<br/> <br/>I would ask; why the colour treatment and why the background? If it''s difficult to answer then it''s probably what many voters were wondering too. I would have chosen pure white or even a very dark colour and shot it from lower down, avoiding the shadows on the background too. This would demonstrate the balance element more dramatically. Since you can''t easily hide the fact that the fork was tucked under the spoon, it could have looked more impressive edge-on (90 degrees). <br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! ...................................................... When viewing challenge images I like to see shots which work outside of the competition - normally a sign that it''s a good shot regardless of votes. This probably suffers in that respect, mainly due to the skewed arrangement, blue colour cast and rather centred composition. The set-up clearly shows balance but it doesn''t engage the viewer much beyond this, which ultimately is what may win votes. I would ask; why the colour treatment and why the background? If it''s difficult to answer then it''s probably what many voters were wondering too. I would have chosen pure white or even a very dark colour and shot it from lower down, avoiding the shadows on the background too. This would demonstrate the balance element more dramatically. Since you can''t easily hide the fact that the fork was tucked under the spoon, it could have looked more impressive edge-on (90 degrees).  CheersJon', '2004-07-29 16:35:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(832258, 91097, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/>......................................................<br/> <br/>While I like this image I do remember wondering why this demonstrated balance particularly - and I also remember thinking that it would look more impressive to show the top of the ball. I had imagined that you had probably kept a finger on the ball (thus rendering the shot quite unimpressive) but I confess to being amazed at the extent of your set-up.<br/> <br/>Technically, it is very well shot, richly coloured and beautifully focused, although this scored higher than I thought it would.<br/> <br/>Personally, more grassy context at the base and the ball top would have made it for me, but since this was not on a golf course you made the best of it. The text on the ball was not a prominent enough feature for me to make it truly ironic, since it really doesn''t matter in golf if the ball isn''t cosmetically aligned on the tee!<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! ...................................................... While I like this image I do remember wondering why this demonstrated balance particularly - and I also remember thinking that it would look more impressive to show the top of the ball. I had imagined that you had probably kept a finger on the ball (thus rendering the shot quite unimpressive) but I confess to being amazed at the extent of your set-up. Technically, it is very well shot, richly coloured and beautifully focused, although this scored higher than I thought it would. Personally, more grassy context at the base and the ball top would have made it for me, but since this was not on a golf course you made the best of it. The text on the ball was not a prominent enough feature for me to make it truly ironic, since it really doesn''t matter in golf if the ball isn''t cosmetically aligned on the tee! CheersJon', '2004-07-29 16:52:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(832926, 91350, 23439, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Imagineer:</b></div><hr/><i> <b>Hello from the Critique Club!</b> <br/>......................................................<br/> <br/>When viewing challenge images I like to see shots which work outside of the competition - normally a sign that it''s a good shot regardless of votes. This probably suffers in that respect, mainly due to the skewed arrangement, blue colour cast and rather centred composition. The set-up clearly shows balance but it doesn''t engage the viewer much beyond this, which ultimately is what may win votes.<br/> <br/>I would ask; why the colour treatment and why the background? If it''s difficult to answer then it''s probably what many voters were wondering too. I would have chosen pure white or even a very dark colour and shot it from lower down, avoiding the shadows on the background too. This would demonstrate the balance element more dramatically. Since you can''t easily hide the fact that the fork was tucked under the spoon, it could have looked more impressive edge-on (90 degrees). <br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the Critique Jon. The reason I chose the background and the color, was because it covered up the crummy setup I had. I wanted a pure white background, but didn''t have what I needed at the time. You''re suggestion of a dark brackground and angle I could''ve done and I think it would have improved the shot a lot. Thanks again.</td>', 'Originally posted by Imagineer: Hello from the Critique Club! ...................................................... When viewing challenge images I like to see shots which work outside of the competition - normally a sign that it''s a good shot regardless of votes. This probably suffers in that respect, mainly due to the skewed arrangement, blue colour cast and rather centred composition. The set-up clearly shows balance but it doesn''t engage the viewer much beyond this, which ultimately is what may win votes. I would ask; why the colour treatment and why the background? If it''s difficult to answer then it''s probably what many voters were wondering too. I would have chosen pure white or even a very dark colour and shot it from lower down, avoiding the shadows on the background too. This would demonstrate the balance element more dramatically. Since you can''t easily hide the fact that the fork was tucked under the spoon, it could have looked more impressive edge-on (90 degrees).  CheersJon   Thanks for the Critique Jon. The reason I chose the background and the color, was because it covered up the crummy setup I had. I wanted a pure white background, but didn''t have what I needed at the time. You''re suggestion of a dark brackground and angle I could''ve done and I think it would have improved the shot a lot. Thanks again.', '2004-07-30 02:34:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(832977, 91686, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Larry from the  Critique Club <br/> <br/>First of all, I like your image although the actual light in the middle makes it a bit harsh, also I  do understand why you would choose this for a Balance Challenge <br/>I myself chose to enter a harmony with nature image with ''My Weta'' but it did badly. I think with this challenge D P C voters have gone more for the actual ''Balance'' theme if you look at the top 10 apart from one they are all objects balancing.<br/> <br/>The greens in your image are very nice and it has a very natural feel to it and all I can really suggest is maybe just tone down the brightness in the image possibly shooting an image like this on a duller day, sunlight is not always the best to produce nice colour in an image ( I find that with my own digital camera )<br/>I am not very technical but I can see that my own camera definately works better on a cloudy day. Sunlight gives the harsh light. <br/>D P C is a great site for learning and it is very difficult to achieve a high standard but it''s always good to try and concentrate on improving our own technique and learning from others I know I have improved  ( not that my score always show that)<br/>But we are all very different and just because the top images get all the votes there is still room for lovely images like yours after all we cannot all be winners can we. I myself would prefer to look at your image to a bunch of objects on top of each other. <br/>But you will also see a very professional approach to an image on this site something I myself find very hard to achieve So keep up the good work and enjoy your photography <br/>I love to go out with my camera and create I think I drive my family nuts! But its a great way to pass the time.<br/>Good luck in the future Challenges I hope my comments help a little and if you have any questions please feel free to P M me<br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Larry from the  Critique Club  First of all, I like your image although the actual light in the middle makes it a bit harsh, also I  do understand why you would choose this for a Balance Challenge I myself chose to enter a harmony with nature image with ''My Weta'' but it did badly. I think with this challenge D P C voters have gone more for the actual ''Balance'' theme if you look at the top 10 apart from one they are all objects balancing. The greens in your image are very nice and it has a very natural feel to it and all I can really suggest is maybe just tone down the brightness in the image possibly shooting an image like this on a duller day, sunlight is not always the best to produce nice colour in an image ( I find that with my own digital camera )I am not very technical but I can see that my own camera definately works better on a cloudy day. Sunlight gives the harsh light. D P C is a great site for learning and it is very difficult to achieve a high standard but it''s always good to try and concentrate on improving our own technique and learning from others I know I have improved  ( not that my score always show that)But we are all very different and just because the top images get all the votes there is still room for lovely images like yours after all we cannot all be winners can we. I myself would prefer to look at your image to a bunch of objects on top of each other. But you will also see a very professional approach to an image on this site something I myself find very hard to achieve So keep up the good work and enjoy your photography I love to go out with my camera and create I think I drive my family nuts! But its a great way to pass the time.Good luck in the future Challenges I hope my comments help a little and if you have any questions please feel free to P M meRegardsSally', '2004-07-30 05:02:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(832981, 92214, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Ken from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>I like your idea and the colour you have chosen  although it looks as if you have chopped a little too much off the bowl <br/>I also think it shows a good balance.<br/> <br/>But with the voters of D P C I think they looked at this challenge as a Balance  of objects challenge if you look at the top 10 at least 9 are objects and are all very good <br/> <br/>I myself did more of a nature harmony image with my Weta and did very poorly also I have critiqued other perfectly lovely images along the same nature theme that only did average to low.<br/> <br/>Maybe the sharper focus suggested in one of your comments would improve the image  but I like it the way it is and the use of the blind behind makes a great background<br/> <br/>So your score is a respectable score and a very worthy image <br/>But as far as I can see not a lot of voters went along with the nature theme so keep trying and enjoy your photography and just know that you have a really nice image <br/>Good luck in your future challenges <br/>I hope my comments help <br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Ken from the Critique Club  I like your idea and the colour you have chosen  although it looks as if you have chopped a little too much off the bowl I also think it shows a good balance. But with the voters of D P C I think they looked at this challenge as a Balance  of objects challenge if you look at the top 10 at least 9 are objects and are all very good  I myself did more of a nature harmony image with my Weta and did very poorly also I have critiqued other perfectly lovely images along the same nature theme that only did average to low. Maybe the sharper focus suggested in one of your comments would improve the image  but I like it the way it is and the use of the blind behind makes a great background So your score is a respectable score and a very worthy image But as far as I can see not a lot of voters went along with the nature theme so keep trying and enjoy your photography and just know that you have a really nice image Good luck in your future challenges I hope my comments help If you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally', '2004-07-30 05:21:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(832984, 91892, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Adine from the Critque Club<br/> <br/>First of all Congratulations on your placing with this image<br/>I just love the blue bottle and it is very nicely done but for my own preference I do not like the brown bottle it has little interest maybe a green one would look better but thats just being very picky<br/>The blue bottle almost looks like its been painted onto a canvas and could almost give the same impact without the smaller bottle <br/>I am not very technical but I can see this is good and very nicely done <br/>Maybe the white background is a little stark or harsh Dark blue maybe would look nice<br/>But other than that I dont really feel I can add a lot more Nice idea well taken<br/>Just keep on enjoying your photography and enjoy D P C I find it very addictive!<br/>I hope my comments although very limited help<br/>If you have any questions feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Adine from the Critque Club First of all Congratulations on your placing with this imageI just love the blue bottle and it is very nicely done but for my own preference I do not like the brown bottle it has little interest maybe a green one would look better but thats just being very pickyThe blue bottle almost looks like its been painted onto a canvas and could almost give the same impact without the smaller bottle I am not very technical but I can see this is good and very nicely done Maybe the white background is a little stark or harsh Dark blue maybe would look niceBut other than that I dont really feel I can add a lot more Nice idea well takenJust keep on enjoying your photography and enjoy D P C I find it very addictive!I hope my comments although very limited helpIf you have any questions feel free to P M me RegardsSally', '2004-07-30 05:30:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(833014, 91549, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Milos from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>I have found as I am going through the critique for this challenge that the ''Nature''theme in Balance has not done very well (My own included really bombed) <br/> <br/>The top 10 images are mostly set up images involving objects and although they are very cleverly done they are just that, set up.<br/>With some really sharp images and great equipment I would imagine <br/> <br/>Your image is not sharply in focus and appears soft but I can see where you are going with the balance theme <br/>The shadows take my eye off the main subject which I assume is the Bumble bee <br/>The Black and white is good but has a blue tinge to it<br/> <br/>I think the main thing with your image in the theme you have chosen (Nature)and your focus, although that can be very hard when taking a photograph of a buzzy bee. It really does need to be sharp Did you have your camera set to Macro?<br/>Enjoy your photography and I hope my comments have helped.<br/>For myself I can get very frustrated with this site when my image gets voted down so low but I put it down to a learning experience <br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me<br/>Good luck in the challenges<br/>Regards<br/>Sally <br/>ps I really like your yellow ribbon image very clever thinking</td>', 'Hello Milos from the Critique Club  I have found as I am going through the critique for this challenge that the ''Nature''theme in Balance has not done very well (My own included really bombed)  The top 10 images are mostly set up images involving objects and although they are very cleverly done they are just that, set up.With some really sharp images and great equipment I would imagine  Your image is not sharply in focus and appears soft but I can see where you are going with the balance theme The shadows take my eye off the main subject which I assume is the Bumble bee The Black and white is good but has a blue tinge to it I think the main thing with your image in the theme you have chosen (Nature)and your focus, although that can be very hard when taking a photograph of a buzzy bee. It really does need to be sharp Did you have your camera set to Macro?Enjoy your photography and I hope my comments have helped.For myself I can get very frustrated with this site when my image gets voted down so low but I put it down to a learning experience If you have any questions please feel free to P M meGood luck in the challengesRegardsSally ps I really like your yellow ribbon image very clever thinking', '2004-07-30 07:11:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(833017, 92123, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Ellen from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>A very clever idea and you certainly have the voters fooled, myself included. Just how did you get that ice so clear?<br/>I like the title and the background but there are a few soft spots maybe it is the cloning or one of the tools you used especially visable on the second row down on the left<br/> <br/>I think a darker background may have given it more of a lift, I think that would make the ice stand out more, maybe a dark or navy blue. but then the title would have to change.  <br/>There are many clever images in this challenge and yours did very well.<br/>So I am not sure what else to say because yours is very clever <br/>( MY own image really bombed) <br/>But keep those ideas coming and enjoy your photography <br/>Good luck in your future challenges <br/>if you have any questions you can P M me<br/>Regards<br/>Sally<br/></td>', 'Hello Ellen from the Critique Club  A very clever idea and you certainly have the voters fooled, myself included. Just how did you get that ice so clear?I like the title and the background but there are a few soft spots maybe it is the cloning or one of the tools you used especially visable on the second row down on the left I think a darker background may have given it more of a lift, I think that would make the ice stand out more, maybe a dark or navy blue. but then the title would have to change.  There are many clever images in this challenge and yours did very well.So I am not sure what else to say because yours is very clever ( MY own image really bombed) But keep those ideas coming and enjoy your photography Good luck in your future challenges if you have any questions you can P M meRegardsSally', '2004-07-30 07:27:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(833024, 92007, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Marac from the  Critique Club <br/> <br/>A very clever and sharp image <br/>colour and tone are excellent<br/>Great idea and I like the shallow D O F <br/>Your score is a decent one so the voters liked it, so nothing to be ashamed of.<br/> <br/>I dont see how you could improve it really<br/>The voters are a fickle lot <br/>I think this sort of image does not appeal to everyone and I feel it would appeal mainly to younger men,( I may be wrong) some may not even know what it is at first glance.<br/>This challenge has some really good images  yours included <br/>So I cannot improve on what you have already done <br/>I hope my comments are helpful to you<br/>Good luck in your future challenges. Enjoy your photography.<br/>If you have any question feel free to P M me<br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Marac from the  Critique Club  A very clever and sharp image colour and tone are excellentGreat idea and I like the shallow D O F Your score is a decent one so the voters liked it, so nothing to be ashamed of. I dont see how you could improve it reallyThe voters are a fickle lot I think this sort of image does not appeal to everyone and I feel it would appeal mainly to younger men,( I may be wrong) some may not even know what it is at first glance.This challenge has some really good images  yours included So I cannot improve on what you have already done I hope my comments are helpful to youGood luck in your future challenges. Enjoy your photography.If you have any question feel free to P M meRegardsSally', '2004-07-30 07:40:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(833375, 92083, 7322, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>This is a very nice idea for the Balance challenge. Overall, I like the composition. The black and deep blue color scheme is very peaceful. I would have to agree with the some of the comments you received during the voting regarding her watch and the sharpness of the image. Something in me feels that the cropping is a bit stingy. I''d like to see a bit more space above her head and at either side. Also, this pose from what is obviously a standing position doesn''t quite portray the meditative quality it would if she were seated. I''m guessing there was no way of achieving this and retaining the simple background of the sky but it appears like more of a set-up this way. <br/> <br/>Still, this is a very good effort and shows that some thought was put into the making of it. <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  This is a very nice idea for the Balance challenge. Overall, I like the composition. The black and deep blue color scheme is very peaceful. I would have to agree with the some of the comments you received during the voting regarding her watch and the sharpness of the image. Something in me feels that the cropping is a bit stingy. I''d like to see a bit more space above her head and at either side. Also, this pose from what is obviously a standing position doesn''t quite portray the meditative quality it would if she were seated. I''m guessing there was no way of achieving this and retaining the simple background of the sky but it appears like more of a set-up this way.  Still, this is a very good effort and shows that some thought was put into the making of it. ', '2004-07-30 12:50:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(833396, 91984, 7322, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>A house of cards is a great subject for illustrating balance. You''ve got a good start here, in spite of your confusing wording. :-D<br/> <br/>I don''t feel that the building of the house is as interesting as the house itself. From this point of view there isn''t really much  to interest the eye. Someone suggested a more dramatic angle of view and I would have to agree with that. I would go further and suggest that the house should have been created in front of a simple background to really highlight the intricate structure. The black vest is a good start but the bright green shirt and background elements of the room are distracting. The wrinkled red cloth adds another overly bright visual element that detracts from the potential elegance of the photo. <br/> <br/>This is the kind of set-up where you could have used a little trickery, such as creating the house of cards with hot glue so that you had a somewhat stable structure that could be moved around a little bit and experimented with. Try different backgrounds using draped cloth, poster board or simply blur the background sufficiently to avoid distracting elements. The angle I would suggest would be from below and from a position which shows the flat planes of some of the cards and not just the edges and negative space. <br/> <br/>This is a fun idea that has a lot of potential for dynamic visual interest.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  A house of cards is a great subject for illustrating balance. You''ve got a good start here, in spite of your confusing wording. :-D I don''t feel that the building of the house is as interesting as the house itself. From this point of view there isn''t really much  to interest the eye. Someone suggested a more dramatic angle of view and I would have to agree with that. I would go further and suggest that the house should have been created in front of a simple background to really highlight the intricate structure. The black vest is a good start but the bright green shirt and background elements of the room are distracting. The wrinkled red cloth adds another overly bright visual element that detracts from the potential elegance of the photo.  This is the kind of set-up where you could have used a little trickery, such as creating the house of cards with hot glue so that you had a somewhat stable structure that could be moved around a little bit and experimented with. Try different backgrounds using draped cloth, poster board or simply blur the background sufficiently to avoid distracting elements. The angle I would suggest would be from below and from a position which shows the flat planes of some of the cards and not just the edges and negative space.  This is a fun idea that has a lot of potential for dynamic visual interest.', '2004-07-30 13:03:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(833473, 91033, 7181, '<td valign="top">Very cool! Well deserved ribbon blue ribbon!</td>', 'Very cool! Well deserved ribbon blue ribbon!', '2004-07-30 13:50:21.000000', NULL, 0),
