(833573, 91987, 24435, '<td valign="top">the couple is so lucky</td>', 'the couple is so lucky', '2004-07-30 14:47:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(833776, 92214, 12962, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by train:</b></div><hr/><i> Hello Ken from the Critique Club <br/> <br/>I like your idea and the colour you have chosen  although it looks as if you have chopped a little too much off the bowl <br/>I also think it shows a good balance.<br/> <br/>But with the voters of D P C I think they looked at this challenge as a Balance  of objects challenge if you look at the top 10 at least 9 are objects and are all very good <br/> <br/>I myself did more of a nature harmony image with my Weta and did very poorly also I have critiqued other perfectly lovely images along the same nature theme that only did average to low.<br/> <br/>Maybe the sharper focus suggested in one of your comments would improve the image  but I like it the way it is and the use of the blind behind makes a great background<br/> <br/>So your score is a respectable score and a very worthy image <br/>But as far as I can see not a lot of voters went along with the nature theme so keep trying and enjoy your photography and just know that you have a really nice image <br/>Good luck in your future challenges <br/>I hope my comments help <br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks, Sally.  I thought that the score was appropriate.  Bonsai is a very specialized process  (where you have to balance everything - nutrients, water, light etc. in order to maintain this miniaturized tree) and also you have to train the tree in such a way that it is balanced in presentation to present harmony of nature and art.  If the reader don''t understand what a bonsai is, then he/she wouldn''t understand where the balance lies.  But I am happy that it did what how it did.  Your critique is certainly very helpful and thanks for the encouragement.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by train: Hello Ken from the Critique Club  I like your idea and the colour you have chosen  although it looks as if you have chopped a little too much off the bowl I also think it shows a good balance. But with the voters of D P C I think they looked at this challenge as a Balance  of objects challenge if you look at the top 10 at least 9 are objects and are all very good  I myself did more of a nature harmony image with my Weta and did very poorly also I have critiqued other perfectly lovely images along the same nature theme that only did average to low. Maybe the sharper focus suggested in one of your comments would improve the image  but I like it the way it is and the use of the blind behind makes a great background So your score is a respectable score and a very worthy image But as far as I can see not a lot of voters went along with the nature theme so keep trying and enjoy your photography and just know that you have a really nice image Good luck in your future challenges I hope my comments help If you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally   Thanks, Sally.  I thought that the score was appropriate.  Bonsai is a very specialized process  (where you have to balance everything - nutrients, water, light etc. in order to maintain this miniaturized tree) and also you have to train the tree in such a way that it is balanced in presentation to present harmony of nature and art.  If the reader don''t understand what a bonsai is, then he/she wouldn''t understand where the balance lies.  But I am happy that it did what how it did.  Your critique is certainly very helpful and thanks for the encouragement.', '2004-07-30 18:37:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(833923, 92206, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>:: Hello from the Critique Club  ::</b> <br/>............................................................<br/> <br/>This is well composed (apart from a tight crop on the right) and it fits the challenge, but it lacks a ''wow factor'' beyond those facts, for which there are some clear reasons:<br/> <br/>The balancing act is not particularly amazing, and the technical execution lacks some finesse. The colours are slightly old fashioned, reminding me of an old English pub with flocked wallpaper and are quite heavy, yielding an oppressive vibe to the image.<br/> <br/>The lighting is harsh and generates many highlights on the glass, and the horizon line is skewed slightly. To aid better lighting on shiney, reflective subjects it is good to aim the light through a diffuser such as a large sheet of paper or a piece of thin cloth. You can also try pointing the light away from the subject and reflecting it back in using a reflector (sometimes the same piece of paper will do) which spreads the point of origin and reduces pinpoint specular highlights.<br/> <br/>Yes - this is picking nits, but in order to score highly, so you will have to too!<br/> <br/>Personally, I would plan to shoot an image that I would be pleased to hang on my wall after the challenge (I frequently fail in this task myself) but it''s something that can help focus one''s objectives prior to deciding on a submission.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', ':: Hello from the Critique Club  :: ............................................................ This is well composed (apart from a tight crop on the right) and it fits the challenge, but it lacks a ''wow factor'' beyond those facts, for which there are some clear reasons: The balancing act is not particularly amazing, and the technical execution lacks some finesse. The colours are slightly old fashioned, reminding me of an old English pub with flocked wallpaper and are quite heavy, yielding an oppressive vibe to the image. The lighting is harsh and generates many highlights on the glass, and the horizon line is skewed slightly. To aid better lighting on shiney, reflective subjects it is good to aim the light through a diffuser such as a large sheet of paper or a piece of thin cloth. You can also try pointing the light away from the subject and reflecting it back in using a reflector (sometimes the same piece of paper will do) which spreads the point of origin and reduces pinpoint specular highlights. Yes - this is picking nits, but in order to score highly, so you will have to too! Personally, I would plan to shoot an image that I would be pleased to hang on my wall after the challenge (I frequently fail in this task myself) but it''s something that can help focus one''s objectives prior to deciding on a submission. CheersJon', '2004-07-30 20:47:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(833930, 92126, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>:: Hello from the Critique Club  ::</b> <br/>............................................................<br/> <br/>As always when shooting for a challenge there is the issue of it <i>obviously</i> meeting the brief for most voters. While I don''t think this does per se, it really doesn''t take much to come round to some form of link to the balance of nature - even without the title. Its weakness may be that there is not much demonstration of the opposite element of the balance, namely the other part of the food chain - the seagulls.<br/> <br/>A short glance at this shot without the benefit of a challenge brief would lead me to think that this is a statement of man''s damage to the environment - of something unnatural, such as posinous waters or pollution.<br/> <br/>Whatever the view on this it''s still an engaging shot, however unpleasant and you were right to retain the colour.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', ':: Hello from the Critique Club  :: ............................................................ As always when shooting for a challenge there is the issue of it obviously meeting the brief for most voters. While I don''t think this does per se, it really doesn''t take much to come round to some form of link to the balance of nature - even without the title. Its weakness may be that there is not much demonstration of the opposite element of the balance, namely the other part of the food chain - the seagulls. A short glance at this shot without the benefit of a challenge brief would lead me to think that this is a statement of man''s damage to the environment - of something unnatural, such as posinous waters or pollution. Whatever the view on this it''s still an engaging shot, however unpleasant and you were right to retain the colour. CheersJon', '2004-07-30 20:54:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(833937, 91033, 22688, '<td valign="top">Sorry to be the one to disagree, but the photo isn''t great.  The "balance" was taken way too literally and creastes a poor and uninteresting composition.  Since when can someone get away with a photo that''s practically symetrical.  Use a little creativity next time. (not deserving of 1st place)<b>text</b></td>', 'Sorry to be the one to disagree, but the photo isn''t great.  The "balance" was taken way too literally and creastes a poor and uninteresting composition.  Since when can someone get away with a photo that''s practically symetrical.  Use a little creativity next time. (not deserving of 1st place)text', '2004-07-30 21:06:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(834051, 91033, 22734, '<td valign="top">Hey Tiger eye: Thank you for your most astute observation. In your cursory look perhaps you overlooked that while the physical balance was symetrical, the abstract is asymetrical. <br/> However, regarding whether it deserved 1st place, about this you may have a point. Following your logic it should not have even been considered for a ribbon. So put me anywhere you seem fit. A composition this bad may be better place around 40th or so.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-08-01 01:05:09.</i></td>', 'Hey Tiger eye: Thank you for your most astute observation. In your cursory look perhaps you overlooked that while the physical balance was symetrical, the abstract is asymetrical.  However, regarding whether it deserved 1st place, about this you may have a point. Following your logic it should not have even been considered for a ribbon. So put me anywhere you seem fit. A composition this bad may be better place around 40th or so.', '2004-07-30 23:12:30.000000', '2004-08-01 02:05:09.000000', 0),
(834227, 92223, 8816, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critque Club!<br/> <br/>First off let me commend you on a great idea, and composition - both are superb and I can think of nothig to change in these departments<br/> <br/>I think one reason this photo lost marks was simply in overall appeal.  While composition was strong, your lighting and coloration lacked something.  I think if you had done this against a simple white or black border the majestic nature of the photo would have been demonstrated clearly, as well as drawing more focus to the bright red strawberry suspended in mid air.  Currently, the bright background downplays the strawberry.<br/> <br/>I think you could have really stunned everyone if you had used selective desaturation here, leaving only the strawberry in color.  This would draw all eyes to the strawberry, which is the main subject of this photo.  From there eyes would see that the strawberry was suspended in mid-air ~place *GASP* of shock here~!<br/> <br/>Personally, I find your lighting to be too noticeably studio type lighting, which distracts from the image, and takes away from the professional quality of the image.  The best way to avoid this is to use natural lighting!  If you had taken this shot outside you could have a) avoided the shadow that falls across the persons hand - and b) had more evenly dispersed light.  Currently the strawberry is a tad blown out on one side, and quite dark on the other.  The strawberry has fantastic detail on it, but it is lost in the lighting you have used.<br/> <br/>Many people mentioned that your photo doesn''t fit the challenge, which in my opinion is a buch of BS!  People really had trouble thinking outside the box on this challenge (look at the challenge winners and it''s quite obvious) so don''t be discouraged!  I think you did a good job of fitting the challenge, actually you managed to do it in two different ways, which is even better!<br/> <br/>In general, I liked your shot.  Your composition and composing idea are strong, especially for the challenge topic, but with a little more time, and some tricks this could''ve really stunned audiences!<br/> <br/>Good Luck!<br/> <br/>Lee</td>', 'Greetings from the Critque Club! First off let me commend you on a great idea, and composition - both are superb and I can think of nothig to change in these departments I think one reason this photo lost marks was simply in overall appeal.  While composition was strong, your lighting and coloration lacked something.  I think if you had done this against a simple white or black border the majestic nature of the photo would have been demonstrated clearly, as well as drawing more focus to the bright red strawberry suspended in mid air.  Currently, the bright background downplays the strawberry. I think you could have really stunned everyone if you had used selective desaturation here, leaving only the strawberry in color.  This would draw all eyes to the strawberry, which is the main subject of this photo.  From there eyes would see that the strawberry was suspended in mid-air ~place *GASP* of shock here~! Personally, I find your lighting to be too noticeably studio type lighting, which distracts from the image, and takes away from the professional quality of the image.  The best way to avoid this is to use natural lighting!  If you had taken this shot outside you could have a) avoided the shadow that falls across the persons hand - and b) had more evenly dispersed light.  Currently the strawberry is a tad blown out on one side, and quite dark on the other.  The strawberry has fantastic detail on it, but it is lost in the lighting you have used. Many people mentioned that your photo doesn''t fit the challenge, which in my opinion is a buch of BS!  People really had trouble thinking outside the box on this challenge (look at the challenge winners and it''s quite obvious) so don''t be discouraged!  I think you did a good job of fitting the challenge, actually you managed to do it in two different ways, which is even better! In general, I liked your shot.  Your composition and composing idea are strong, especially for the challenge topic, but with a little more time, and some tricks this could''ve really stunned audiences! Good Luck! Lee', '2004-07-31 03:53:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(834275, 92010, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Sue from the Critique Club : <br/> <br/>First of all I know just how difficult these little birds are to photograph I have been trying for ages and have three resonable images  but I have yet to get it with its tail spread out. <br/>They are beautiful but very fidgety birds and they always follow us when we take our dog for a walk.<br/>I like the inclusion of the spiders web but I think your image would look better if it was cropped closer and not so central Photographers seem to have a ''thing'' about being off centre  but I think it looks alright the way you have it, although the post is a bit heavy looking behind such a fragile bird <br/>Colour tones are nice, the focus on the fantail could be a little sharper  and your score in the challenge did far better than my own <br/>But to make this more of a ''Wow'' image I think it needs a sharper focus and maybe the little fantail could look less ruffled. LOL! <br/> <br/>I have critiqued quite a few of the Nature images in the Balance challenge and they did not do as well as the objects balancing just look at the top 10 images  although for my own interest I like the nature ones the best thats not to take anything away from the top 10 they are all brilliant <br/>Sorry I cant be more of a help <br/>Enjoy your photography and good luck in the future Challenges <br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally Incidentially I live in Hamilton New Zealand <br/></td>', 'Hello Sue from the Critique Club :  First of all I know just how difficult these little birds are to photograph I have been trying for ages and have three resonable images  but I have yet to get it with its tail spread out. They are beautiful but very fidgety birds and they always follow us when we take our dog for a walk.I like the inclusion of the spiders web but I think your image would look better if it was cropped closer and not so central Photographers seem to have a ''thing'' about being off centre  but I think it looks alright the way you have it, although the post is a bit heavy looking behind such a fragile bird Colour tones are nice, the focus on the fantail could be a little sharper  and your score in the challenge did far better than my own But to make this more of a ''Wow'' image I think it needs a sharper focus and maybe the little fantail could look less ruffled. LOL!  I have critiqued quite a few of the Nature images in the Balance challenge and they did not do as well as the objects balancing just look at the top 10 images  although for my own interest I like the nature ones the best thats not to take anything away from the top 10 they are all brilliant Sorry I cant be more of a help Enjoy your photography and good luck in the future Challenges If you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally Incidentially I live in Hamilton New Zealand ', '2004-07-31 06:06:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(834625, 92084, 22734, '<td valign="top">From the critique club:<br/> <br/>A very interesting vertical composition. At first glance it illicits a very up feeling. Its object being more to entertain and please the eyes. The dark background, whether used to hold the composition, makes the colors reach out and give it that almost pop art feel. This picture simply makes the viewer smile. Disguised behind the expert lighting and above average composition is a healthy sense of humor which comes right true on first glance.<br/>  In real life, the structure would fall, but your object here was not to actually balance the fruits but to hint at the thought. Yet, it is this approach which gives it that odd comical look. You take care, minus the typo, to infer the nutritional balance and you have succeeded.<br/>  Since the composition is strong and the lighting is superb, there is nothing that I can add here. In particular you did a superb job with the lightinhg. The highlights preserve the texture and the fading into dark at the bottom gives it that artistic touch. Above all, you have made the fruit and the colors very true. This is a worthy addition to any port. In short, it is beyond my criticism. dan<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-07-31 17:17:55.</i></td>', 'From the critique club: A very interesting vertical composition. At first glance it illicits a very up feeling. Its object being more to entertain and please the eyes. The dark background, whether used to hold the composition, makes the colors reach out and give it that almost pop art feel. This picture simply makes the viewer smile. Disguised behind the expert lighting and above average composition is a healthy sense of humor which comes right true on first glance.  In real life, the structure would fall, but your object here was not to actually balance the fruits but to hint at the thought. Yet, it is this approach which gives it that odd comical look. You take care, minus the typo, to infer the nutritional balance and you have succeeded.  Since the composition is strong and the lighting is superb, there is nothing that I can add here. In particular you did a superb job with the lightinhg. The highlights preserve the texture and the fading into dark at the bottom gives it that artistic touch. Above all, you have made the fruit and the colors very true. This is a worthy addition to any port. In short, it is beyond my criticism. dan', '2004-07-31 18:15:40.000000', '2004-07-31 18:17:55.000000', 0),
(835017, 92155, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Carol From the  Critique Club <br/> <br/>I really like your image and your choice for B/W<br/>very good detail, focus is sharp and I think this says ''balance'' you could try and tone down the middle tones to make it a bit darker as suggested in one of your comments  but I like the way it is.  <br/> <br/>In the critiques Iv''e done so far I am finding that the ''nature'' images didn''t do so well in this ''Balance'' challenge if you look at the top 10 images they are all objects balancing with the exception of one.<br/> <br/>So in my opinion there is nothing I can see that is obviously wrong with your image it''s just a matter of the voters went for the objects <br/> <br/>In my own nature Balance entry I did very badly but I thought the Weta was in harmonious surroundings creating a balance but the voters didnt see that.<br/> <br/>So we live and learn, I guess.<br/> <br/> I liked the winners image very sharp and cleverly done  but I''d rather look at an image like yours anyday so I find it very hard to say anything objective ( if I am real picky maybe the white on the stem on the top left flower is a bit harsh near the top and could be cloned out)<br/> <br/>You had an average score for an image that will be liked by a whole lot of people myself included.<br/>But for D P C ribbon winners, I think, they just have an edge somehow <br/>I''d like to know how they do it!  <br/> <br/>I hope that you enjoy your photography. Good luck in the future challenges. I hope my comments help and if you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Carol From the  Critique Club  I really like your image and your choice for B/Wvery good detail, focus is sharp and I think this says ''balance'' you could try and tone down the middle tones to make it a bit darker as suggested in one of your comments  but I like the way it is.   In the critiques Iv''e done so far I am finding that the ''nature'' images didn''t do so well in this ''Balance'' challenge if you look at the top 10 images they are all objects balancing with the exception of one. So in my opinion there is nothing I can see that is obviously wrong with your image it''s just a matter of the voters went for the objects  In my own nature Balance entry I did very badly but I thought the Weta was in harmonious surroundings creating a balance but the voters didnt see that. So we live and learn, I guess.  I liked the winners image very sharp and cleverly done  but I''d rather look at an image like yours anyday so I find it very hard to say anything objective ( if I am real picky maybe the white on the stem on the top left flower is a bit harsh near the top and could be cloned out) You had an average score for an image that will be liked by a whole lot of people myself included.But for D P C ribbon winners, I think, they just have an edge somehow I''d like to know how they do it!   I hope that you enjoy your photography. Good luck in the future challenges. I hope my comments help and if you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally', '2004-08-01 03:33:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(835019, 92220, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Harvey from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>This is an unusual image. A great find. I really like the movment of the birds against the blue sky. Image is sharp and well composed although if you could have included the bottom of the birds in the shot I think it would look better than cropped off.<br/> <br/> I am not sure about the Plane but it does add to the balance theme I guess.I think I would have cloned the plane out but then that''s just a matter of preference I dont suppose you will appreciate my comment if you had been waiting a long time for that jet LOL!<br/> <br/>You had a good score and nothing to be ashamed of here. A worthy image and I really cannot add much more  I hope that you continue to enjoy your photography and Good luck in the future challenges<br/>If you have any questions please feel free to PM me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally<br/></td>', 'Hello Harvey from the Critique Club This is an unusual image. A great find. I really like the movment of the birds against the blue sky. Image is sharp and well composed although if you could have included the bottom of the birds in the shot I think it would look better than cropped off.  I am not sure about the Plane but it does add to the balance theme I guess.I think I would have cloned the plane out but then that''s just a matter of preference I dont suppose you will appreciate my comment if you had been waiting a long time for that jet LOL! You had a good score and nothing to be ashamed of here. A worthy image and I really cannot add much more  I hope that you continue to enjoy your photography and Good luck in the future challengesIf you have any questions please feel free to PM me RegardsSally', '2004-08-01 03:45:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(835041, 92076, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Ruedi from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>This is a good image in itself and a good choice for Balance<br/>with the Silver and red ball and the man with the crutches both showing balance <br/>The image is a little dark but the man has nice skin tone. Good sharp focus.<br/> <br/>I really don''t know what to say about why it didnt do any better in the Challenge. I guess its the sort of image that either appeals or doesn''t and I don''t think that''s any reflection on you as a photographer <br/>D P C has very hard to please voters in my opinion LOL! I am still trying hard to please them but getting nowhere lately!<br/> <br/>Enjoy your photography and I hope you do well in future challenges<br/>I hope my comments are helpful to you and if you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Ruedi from the Critique Club This is a good image in itself and a good choice for Balancewith the Silver and red ball and the man with the crutches both showing balance The image is a little dark but the man has nice skin tone. Good sharp focus. I really don''t know what to say about why it didnt do any better in the Challenge. I guess its the sort of image that either appeals or doesn''t and I don''t think that''s any reflection on you as a photographer D P C has very hard to please voters in my opinion LOL! I am still trying hard to please them but getting nowhere lately! Enjoy your photography and I hope you do well in future challengesI hope my comments are helpful to you and if you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally', '2004-08-01 04:51:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(835051, 92212, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Tim From the Critique Club <br/> <br/>This is a great action shot with lots going on and a good capture <br/>and definately fits the challenge <br/>It may be improved if it was a little bit sharper but the amount of surf seems to almost overtake the image <br/>Maybe the mid tonal levels reduced would make it darker and bring the subject out more I am not very technical though <br/>But overall a really good subject which gave you a really decent score and its so frustrating when a shot like this is overlooked because of some great ''set up'' images. I can imagine your image  would be quite hard to achieve and you would feel really great about putting up for the challenge only to be out voted.<br/>But it is a natural image with good detail so just enjoy your photography and keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>D P C voters are a hard to please lot. LOL! <br/>Good luck in future Challenges and if you have any questions plese feel free to P M me <br/>Regards <br/>Sally</td>', 'Hello Tim From the Critique Club  This is a great action shot with lots going on and a good capture and definately fits the challenge It may be improved if it was a little bit sharper but the amount of surf seems to almost overtake the image Maybe the mid tonal levels reduced would make it darker and bring the subject out more I am not very technical though But overall a really good subject which gave you a really decent score and its so frustrating when a shot like this is overlooked because of some great ''set up'' images. I can imagine your image  would be quite hard to achieve and you would feel really great about putting up for the challenge only to be out voted.But it is a natural image with good detail so just enjoy your photography and keep up the good work. D P C voters are a hard to please lot. LOL! Good luck in future Challenges and if you have any questions plese feel free to P M me Regards Sally', '2004-08-01 05:06:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(835059, 91222, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Amanda from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>This is a very well ''Balanced'' and thought out image, and beautifully presented.<br/> <br/>The focus, detail,colour and tone are all excellent.<br/> <br/>Your score is very good also<br/>I cannot add anymore to it really or  make any improvements <br/>The D P C voters are a hard lot to please. LOL! <br/>There were a lot of good images in the Balance Challenge, yours being one of them. I guess the problem is there can be only 3 winners <br/> <br/>Enjoy your photography <br/>Good luck with your future challenges<br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally <br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello Amanda from the Critique Club This is a very well ''Balanced'' and thought out image, and beautifully presented. The focus, detail,colour and tone are all excellent. Your score is very good alsoI cannot add anymore to it really or  make any improvements The D P C voters are a hard lot to please. LOL! There were a lot of good images in the Balance Challenge, yours being one of them. I guess the problem is there can be only 3 winners  Enjoy your photography Good luck with your future challengesIf you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally  ', '2004-08-01 05:17:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(835065, 90674, 11078, '<td valign="top">Hello Lee from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>This is a very nice image, but I think your score suffered from the fact that this type of image has been used before. Not original in itself although you have used sheet music instead of a book but first glance voters would not notice that ( In my opinion) <br/>Lighting at the top of the page seems too bright and this may be helped by lowering your mid tonal values.<br/> <br/>Apart from the top third which is soft but that could be intentional <br/>It has very good focus and colour <br/>The image fits the challenge <br/>Almost got the score you wanted and maybe like I said before if this had been the first time with this type of image it would have done better <br/>So enjoy your photography <br/>Good luck in future Challenges<br/>If you have any questions please feel free to P M me <br/>Regards<br/>Sally <br/></td>', 'Hello Lee from the Critique Club This is a very nice image, but I think your score suffered from the fact that this type of image has been used before. Not original in itself although you have used sheet music instead of a book but first glance voters would not notice that ( In my opinion) Lighting at the top of the page seems too bright and this may be helped by lowering your mid tonal values. Apart from the top third which is soft but that could be intentional It has very good focus and colour The image fits the challenge Almost got the score you wanted and maybe like I said before if this had been the first time with this type of image it would have done better So enjoy your photography Good luck in future ChallengesIf you have any questions please feel free to P M me RegardsSally ', '2004-08-01 05:30:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(835074, 92168, 8816, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critque Club!<br/> <br/>This seems to be another case where marks were taken due to the fact the image appears to be more of a snapshot then purposefully composed photograph.  The image lacks that something special that a viewer looks for.<br/> <br/>The t-shirt background is one major cause of this problem.  The logo on the t-shirt draws attention away from the subject of the picture - the ice cream cones.  If you were determined to use a t-shirt background perhaps a flat color would have worked better, as it would provide a uncluttered background where the ice cream cones would have jumped out, providing clear subject-matter.<br/> <br/>The lighting used in the photo is quite harsh, and does not light the ice cream cones in a pleasing way.  You mention that you used flash to lighten the picture.  Flash is a tricky tool to use, especially when lighting an image from directly in front.  It gives them a very flat look, and can cast bad shadows directly behind the subject, distracting the viewer.  I would have recommended shooting this outside using natural light.  Or indoors with some good 3-point lighting.  This would have ensured a good, solid form of lighting that gives an interesting 3 dimensional view of the cones.<br/> <br/>Personally, I would have done the photo against a black background, with  3 point lighting set up to illuminate the cones and the hands, and nothing else.  I would have also liked to see the cones closer together and a much closer crop.  This may have given the photo that little thing that makes it special, and please the viewer.<br/> <br/>Overall, I think your idea had merit.  It was a bit of stretch to fit the challenge, but it did so in a humorous way.  Knowing your work GeneralE, it seems like you were a bit rushed putting this together and couldn''t put everything into it.<br/> <br/>Good Luck!<br/> <br/>Lee</td>', 'Greetings from the Critque Club! This seems to be another case where marks were taken due to the fact the image appears to be more of a snapshot then purposefully composed photograph.  The image lacks that something special that a viewer looks for. The t-shirt background is one major cause of this problem.  The logo on the t-shirt draws attention away from the subject of the picture - the ice cream cones.  If you were determined to use a t-shirt background perhaps a flat color would have worked better, as it would provide a uncluttered background where the ice cream cones would have jumped out, providing clear subject-matter. The lighting used in the photo is quite harsh, and does not light the ice cream cones in a pleasing way.  You mention that you used flash to lighten the picture.  Flash is a tricky tool to use, especially when lighting an image from directly in front.  It gives them a very flat look, and can cast bad shadows directly behind the subject, distracting the viewer.  I would have recommended shooting this outside using natural light.  Or indoors with some good 3-point lighting.  This would have ensured a good, solid form of lighting that gives an interesting 3 dimensional view of the cones. Personally, I would have done the photo against a black background, with  3 point lighting set up to illuminate the cones and the hands, and nothing else.  I would have also liked to see the cones closer together and a much closer crop.  This may have given the photo that little thing that makes it special, and please the viewer. Overall, I think your idea had merit.  It was a bit of stretch to fit the challenge, but it did so in a humorous way.  Knowing your work GeneralE, it seems like you were a bit rushed putting this together and couldn''t put everything into it. Good Luck! Lee', '2004-08-01 06:05:30.000000', NULL, 0),
